{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Do not be hasty.</b> Let not your mouth bring forth words before Him in His house in haste, and let not your heart rush—for should you grasp Him, the heart is as one lost and wandering in worldly affairs. Therefore the anointed of YHWH said: \"Your servant has found his heart\" (II Samuel 7:27). And know: Elohim stands over you, sees you, and hears your words, for He is in heaven, in the height of the highest heights, while you are on earth, and there is nothing lower than you. Therefore let your words be few, so you not come to harm—just as the High Priest on the Day of Atonement would pray a short prayer and depart." "Said Abraham the author: Behold, I have ventured to speak. For because the glory of the Place fills every place, and man cannot guard himself in every place, a place was prepared for him, fixed for his prayer, and he is obligated to honor it. A man is also obligated to give thanks and praise to his Elohim at every moment, for His kindness is upon him in every fraction of a moment, keeping him alive and giving him pleasure through sensation. Only because man is occupied with worldly affairs were set times established for him to pray—known times, evening and morning and noon—for whoever has eyes knows the time of the sun's rising, its setting, and its arrival. Therefore a man is obligated to pray, to guard the openings of his mouth, and to consider in his heart that he stands before a King in whose hand is life and death. Therefore it is forbidden for a man to pray and introduce piyyutim into his prayer whose core meaning he does not know, nor should he rely on the author's original intent—for there is no man who does not sin, or the copyists erred." "And as a general rule I say: in the piyyutim of Rabbi Elazar ha-Kalir, may his rest be honorable, there are four difficult things. The first: most of his piyyutim are riddles and parables. I shall mention one of his piyyutim, namely: \"ליראי יקפיל, וחדשים יכפיל, ליום זה פור הפיל, ומציון ימלוך.\" Some interpret \"ליראי\" with a yod, meaning the Messiah will fold the earth before him and fold months, doubling the months of the years to hasten his coming. Others say it is without a yod, meaning \"to remove,\" like the word \"ראי דרכך בגי\" (Jeremiah 2:23). \"וחדשים יכפיל\" means \"he will hide\"—and they are the new constellations that recently arrived." "And the sages of the generation interpret: \"ליראי יקפיל\"—that He will remove these heavens, which are likened to a cast mirror. \"וחדשים יכפיל\"—these are the new heavens. This too is incorrect, for the language yields nothing other than: He will remove a mirror and double months. Perhaps they are thick ropes and He will double them, so the blow with them will be severe? Would a person say: \"There is none among all the living as wise as עַיִר,\" and claim its meaning is \"man,\" because it is said: \"ועיר פרא אדם יולד\" (Job 11:12)? Or say: \"Happy is the people that serves fire and bows down to the sun,\" and claim that \"fire\" is the honored and awesome Name, because it is said: \"כי ה' אלהיך אש אוכלה הוא אל קנא\" (Deuteronomy 4:24), and He is also \"sun,\" as it is said: \"כי שמש ומגן ה'\"? Even though these are likeness without a kaf, like a strong mirror. And Scripture did not liken the skies to a mirror except in strength. Would a person pray: \"Blessed are You, a lion,\" because it is found in that place: \"כאריה ישאג\" (Hosea 11:10)? Would it be good in the eyes of YHWH? And why should we not learn from Solomon, than whom no wise man came after him—behold, his prayer that he prayed is known, and everyone who knows the holy language understands it, and it is not riddles and parables. And likewise the prayer of Daniel, who was a minister. Behold, these prayed only in clear words, for they were wise—even though one who prays on behalf of many people, and all of them are not wise. And likewise every prayer for weekday and holy day that the early ones established—there are no riddles and parables in it. And what sense is there in doubling the new heavens? Were they twofold, that He should double them?" "And to say about the Place: \"ליום זה פור הפיל\"—this is not correct, for one who casts a lot does not know what will be. And he should have fled from the expression \"פור הפיל,\" for it is found only in the context of the enemy. Moreover: after He removes these heavens and earth, how will He rule from Zion, when it is part of the earth?" "One of the sages of the generation answered and said that the rhyme of \"יקפיל\" required him to say \"פור הפיל.\" I replied to him that we do not find the prophets in all their prayer occupying themselves with rhyme. Moreover, he could have built on a different rhyme. And why ride on an elephant? And that one—will it knock to the ground! And if he saw in a dream that he would make a rhyme on an elephant, and upon waking needed to interpret his dream—he should say: \"לוחץ יעפיל, להתנשא יפיל, ורמי לב ישפיל, ומציון ימלוך.\"" "And the second: his piyyutim are mixed with Talmudic language. It is known that there are several forms in the Talmud that are not the holy language. As they said: \"Scriptural language is one thing, and Talmudic language is another\" (Avodah Zarah 58b). And who brought us into this trouble, to pray in foreign tongues? Did not Nehemiah rebuke those who spoke Ashdodite!—all the more so during prayer. And why should we not learn from the fixed prayer, which is entirely pure diction in the holy language? And why should we pray in Median, Persian, Edomite, and Ishmaelite?" "And the third: even the words that are in the holy language—there are great errors in them, such as \"אנסיכה מלכי לפניו.\" What is found of this matter: \"ואני נסכתי מלכי\" (Psalms 2:6)—and it is from the kal binyan, on the pattern of \"נפלתי\" and \"נדרתי,\" and the future is \"אסוך\" or \"אנסוך,\" like \"אפול\" and \"אדור.\" From the heavy binyan one says \"הפיל,\" and the future \"אפיל\"; and likewise from \"הסיך\" one says \"אסיך,\" or \"אנסיך\" when the nun appears, like \"ולנפיל ירך\" (Numbers 5:22). Behold, the meaning of \"אנסיכה מלכי\" would be: \"I will make a libation of him,\" like: \"בל אסיך נסכיהם מדם\" (Psalms 16:4). And some interpret \"נסכתי מלכי\" as \"I anointed.\" And if this interpretation were true, it would be an error. Secondly: why did he not say \"ארומם מלכי\" or \"אשבח ואודה,\" or \"אקדש\"? He only sought a word to display his wisdom to the listeners." "And we are obligated to know the grammar of the language well, so that we not err—like those who bless the Grace after Meals and say \"זְננו,\" not knowing that \"זננו\" is from זנה like \"עננו\" is from ענה; and from זן one should say only \"זוּננו,\" like \"שובנו אלהי ישענו.\"" "And sometimes one says \"צו\" as past tense, when it is in fact imperative, as many say in a fast: \"שֶחָל עניו פניך.\" It is known that the shin stands in place of \"אשר,\" like \"מה שהיה הוא שיהיה\"; and \"חל\" is imperative for the future, like: \"חל נא את פני ה'.\" The correct form is \"שֶחִלָּה,\" like \"ובהכנעו חלה את פני ה'.\"" "Moreover: the holy language is, in the hands of Rabbi Elazar, may his memory be for blessing [?], like a breached city without a wall—he makes masculine forms feminine and vice versa. He said: \"שושן עמק אויימה.\" It is known that the heh of \"שושנה\" is feminine, and the heh reverts to a tav when in construct: \"שושנת העמקים.\" And when the heh or tav is removed—it becomes masculine, like \"צדקה\" and \"צדק.\" How can he say of \"שושן\"—\"אויימה\"? And why did he flee from the verse and not say: \"שושנת עמק אויימה\"? Moreover: what business does a lily have that he should describe it with awe? Was the lily frightened? The only descriptions of a lily are plucked, or fresh, or withered." "One of the sages of the generation said: he needed to say \"אויימה\" because of the richness of its rhyme. I replied: if this is a rich rhyme, then there are in his piyyutim rhymes that are poor and destitute, begging at doors—for he joined \"הר\" with \"נבחר.\" If it is because both are from guttural letters, then let him also join alef and ayin with them. And with bet and vav—for he also joins \"לוי\" with \"נביא\"—let him join mem and peh with them, and all the rhymes will be five, like the number of the letter-outputs. And if the reason for joining heh with chet is that their forms are close in writing, then let him join resh with dalet—even though we find \"דעואל\" / \"רעואל,\" \"דודנים\" / \"רודנים.\" And likewise let him join \"משפטים\" with \"פתים,\" for they are from one output, and tav is found in place of tet in the word \"נצטדק,\" \"הצטיידנו,\" \"ויצטירו.\" And likewise he joined \"ויום\" with \"פדיון\" and \"עליון\"—this too is incorrect, even though mem is found in place of nun, like \"חיין\" and \"חטין\"—how can he swap the mem of \"יום,\" which is a root letter, with the nun of \"עליון\" and \"פדיון,\" which derive from \"עלה\" and \"פדה,\" and it is not a root letter? Moreover, what is the purpose of rhyme? Only that it be pleasant to the ear, so that it senses that the ending of this is like the ending of that. And perhaps he had some intuition of a sixth sense, that mem is like nun, though they are not from one output. Furthermore, he joined \"עושר\" with \"עשר תעשר\"—this too is incorrect, unless the one praying were an Ephrathite who confuses shin and samekh." "There are those who say: one does not answer the lion after his death. The reply: a divine spirit made us all, and the material from which the ancients were formed is like ours, and the ear tests words. And we all know that Daniel was a prophet and master over all the diviners and sages of Babylon—and behold, our sages of blessed memory said: Daniel erred in his calculation—and calculation is a simple matter. Moreover, Jeremiah the prophet was in the time of Daniel. And after our sages demonstrated the proof of his error—will they say to them: if Daniel had been alive, would he have misled the one who misled him? And others said: HaShem wants the heart. If so, why do we need to speak at all, for He knows the secrets of the heart? Did they not enact for the ancients to say on the fast of Yom Kippur: \"May it be Your will... that slanderers have no sway over Your people Israel, and let them not stumble with their tongues\"?" "And the fourth: all his piyyutim are full of midrashim and aggadot. And our sages said: \"A verse does not depart from its plain sense.\" If so, it is not fitting to pray except according to the peshat, and not according to a way that has a sod, or that is in the manner of a parable, or whose legal ruling is not followed, or that may be interpreted in many directions. Do we not know from the midrash on the Song of Songs that every \"Solomon\" mentioned there is sacred, and its meaning is: the King to whom peace belongs. Would it be proper for a man to say in his prayer: \"Save me, King Solomon\"?" "Now we find in Scripture those who say: \"מה משא ה'\"—and they knew that there is משא like prophecy. And those who spoke to Jeremiah the prophet, \"מה משא ה'\"—the prophet did not know their secret, that they meant nothing other than the sense of \"each man concerning his burden.\" And He who knows hidden things told the prophet to say to them: that the burden shall be to the man of His word, and \"you have perverted the words\" etc., \"surely you will say, what is the word of YHWH and what has YHWH answered\"—so there is no ambiguity in the matter. And likewise: \"לא תקראו לי עוד בעלי\"—but He is \"בעל נעורים\" like \"אישי.\" Therefore, because the word בעל is ambiguous with Baal, who is an idol, the prophet said: even a word in which there is ambiguity akin to the matter of an idol you shall not say concerning me, but rather \"אישי.\"" "And the Gaon [Saadia] guarded himself against these four things in his two petitionary prayers, which no author composed like them—they are in biblical language and precise grammar, without riddles and parables and without derash. And there was a sage in France, who also composed a petition beginning \"אמרי האזינה ה',\" and there are things in it that are not correct. Among them he said: \"הרחבת עולמות אין להם גבול\"—and that which has no limit is not created. And he said: \"כי שמך בך ובך שמך\"—had he known the meaning of שֵם, this statement would not have arisen in his heart. Moreover, after he said \"כי שמך בך\"—is it not itself \"בך שמך\"? For what is the difference between \"שלום עליך\" or \"עליך שלום,\" \"ראובן אתה\" or \"אתה ראובן,\" \"תפשום חיים\" or \"חיים תפשום,\" \"יברכך ה' וישמרך\" / \"ה' ישמרך מכל רע\"? And this is not the way of prayer but the way of mockery. And a certain sage who joined two words and made them one in his mouth, like \"אברמל\"—meaning: \"I will explain a word\" or \"I will verify a word.\" Behold, he did the opposite of what he said, for he neither explained the word nor spoke clearly—he only garbled the language, the holy language." "And I cannot explain one of a thousand of the errors of the paytanim. And what seems good to me is that a man not pray with them, but rather with the fixed prayer—and let our words be few, that we not be punished in judgment."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {10 "Resolved the abbreviation נ\"ע as 'may his memory be for blessing,' though the standard expansion is 'may his rest be in Eden'; the context of critique makes the intent uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:49:34.660963013Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}